.he "format (1)                 11/6/26                  format (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
format
- format a hard disk or floppy diskette
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
format [ -m model ] [ -c cylinders ] [ -h heads ] [ -s sectors ]
[ -P precomp ] [ -l lowcur ] [ -d step ] [ -k skew ]
[ -p ] [ -t track ] [ -e head ] device
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Format lays down the low-level shape of a disk and writes the label a
fresh drive needs.  It is one program for the disk controllers this
tree formats, and it reads which controller from the device node: the
high byte of the node's device number is the major, sys/consts.c's
biosw.  The drive and slice come from the low byte, as they do for
read and write.  The device must be the drive's whole-disk slice,
the
.bd
c
node, for every controller but the floppy: block 0 of that slice is
physical cylinder 0 head 0 sector 0, which is where the label goes and
the only address that can be found before the label has been read.
.sp
What the tool does for each controller is what that controller can do:
.sp
.in +10
.ti -10
HDCA	The port-mapped Winchester controller of the M10, M20 and M26.
Format writes a sector header for every sector of every head of every
track, in the controller's own interleave.  The data fields are not
touched, so a drive that already carries a label keeps its table; a
fresh drive gets the prototype table.
.sp
.ti -10
HD-DMA	The Morrow disk controller.  Format loads the drive's constants,
calibrates, and writes a track and a head at a time, filling every data
field with 0xe5.  The label and its table are written afterwards, because
the format has just erased the sector they live in.
.sp
.ti -10
SCSI	The target does the work: FORMAT UNIT blanks the drive, and format
writes the label and the prototype table.
.sp
.ti -10
IDE	The IDE card has no format command in this tree.  Format writes
the label - a fresh or uncarved drive gets the prototype table, a
carved one keeps its own - which is what label -w does for this card.
.sp
.ti -10
DJ-DMA	The 8 inch floppy: 77 tracks, track 0 single density with 26
sectors of 128 bytes and tracks 1 through 76 double density with 15
sectors of 512 bytes.  It writes tracks only and no label, so a
diskette has nothing for mkfs to read until a labeler or mkbootimg has
put a label in block 0.
.sp
.in -10
The geometry comes from the disk's own label, unless a model name or a
flag says otherwise: a model overrides the label, and -c, -h and -s
override the model.  A model name is looked up in the table belonging to the
controller the device names, so
.bd
m10
is the Seagate ST-412 on an HD-DMA and the Discus M10 on an HDCA.  With
no model, no label and no flags there is no geometry, and format
refuses to guess.
.sp
.in +10
.ti -10
-m model	The drive model.  On the HD-DMA the names are m5, m10, m16,
m32 and m40, and the xt, st, ms, tm, q, wren and mp drives.  On the HDCA
they are m10, m20 and m26.  The SCSI, IDE and floppy controllers have no
model table.
.sp
.ti -10
-c cylinders	The track count, overriding the model's or the label's.
.sp
.ti -10
-h heads	The head count.
.sp
.ti -10
-s sectors	The sectors (512-byte blocks) per track.
.sp
.ti -10
-P precomp	The cylinder where write precompensation begins (HD-DMA).
.sp
.ti -10
-l lowcur	The cylinder where the write current drops to its low
setting (HD-DMA).
.sp
.ti -10
-d step	The step pulse delay in 100 microseconds (HD-DMA).
.sp
.ti -10
-k skew	The interleave, the order the sectors are laid down (HD-DMA).
.sp
.ti -10
-p	Probe the drive rather than format it (HDCA).  The probe reads
the sector headers to find the drive's cylinders, heads and sectors per
track, and prints its capacity.
.sp
.ti -10
-t track	Format one cylinder (HD-DMA) or one track (DJ-DMA) instead
of the whole drive.  This is a repair, not a statement about the
drive's shape.  The HDCA, the 5380 and the IDE card have no partial
format.  A bare track number after the device is the same as -t; that
is djformat's old form.
.sp
.ti -10
-e head	Format one head of every cylinder (HD-DMA).  A partial HD-DMA
walk rewrites the label only if it crossed cylinder 0 head 0.
.sp
.in -10
A geometry from no model, no label and no flags is an error.  The label
cannot hold more than 65535 cylinders, 255 heads or 255 sectors; the
HD-DMA selects at most sixteen heads, and the HDCA allows 255, 8 and 32.
An option a controller does not use is an error rather than ignored, so
a mistaken drive is not formatted with a shape nobody asked for.
.sp
The label is written at byte 256 of block 0.  It carries the geometry,
the first sector number the controller uses - one for the HDCA, zero
for the other fixed disks; a floppy's label, where it has one, is the
medium's own - the drive's timing on the HD-DMA, and the drive's
manufacturer and model.  A fresh or uncarved label gets the prototype
table: cylinder 0 for the boot and the label,
.bd
a
for the largest filesystem that fits,
.bd
b
for 1M of swap, and d through h for further filesystems.  A carved
table is kept when the format did not blank the data fields.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
EXAMPLE
.in +5
.br
.bd
format -m m40 /dev/hd0c
.sp
format -m m26 /dev/hdac
.sp
format -c 392 -h 16 -s 63 /dev/scsi0c
.sp
format -c 392 -h 16 -s 63 /dev/ide0c
.sp
format -p /dev/hdac
.sp
format /dev/fla
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
label (1), mkfs (1), mwformat (1), hdformat (1), scsiformat (1),
djformat (1)
